<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Republican People's Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CHP, Cumhuriyet Halk Partisi, People's Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Republican People's Party (CHP) is the oldest political party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>. It grew out of the resistance groups of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>, united at the 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> Congress, and was founded as the People's Party in 1923. In the one-party period it carried out sweeping reforms, among them strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secularism">secularism</a>, women's suffrage and the switch of written Turkish to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script, while suppressing opposition and Kurdish resistance to assimilation. Its logo, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-six-arrows">Six Arrows</a>, stands for the principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kemalism">Kemalism</a>. After losing the 1950 election it handed over power peacefully; banned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> of 1980, it was re-established in 1992.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party has its origins in the Defence of Rights Associations, the resistance organisations created in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. At the Sivas Congress, Mustafa Kemal Pasha and his colleagues united them into the Association for the Defence of National Rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and Rumelia. Most of its members had earlier been Young Turks associated with the Committee of Union and Progress. The party counts the Sivas Congress as its first party congress. It was founded as the People's Party in 1923 and took the prefix Republican shortly after the proclamation of the Republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1924 a right-wing opposition led by Kâzım Karabekir, reacting against the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>, formed the Progressive Republican Party. It was accused of involvement in the Sheikh Said rebellion and of conspiring to assassinate Atatürk. Martial law was declared, every party but the CHP was banned and Karabekir's supporters were purged from government. Republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> became the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> of interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>. After the Second World War İsmet İnönü allowed multi-party elections. The CHP lost in 1950 and stayed in opposition for ten years, during which it began a long shift towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a>.</p><p>Under Bülent Ecevit, chairman from 1972, the party took a left-wing course, and in 1977 it won 41 per cent of the vote, its best result in a free multi-party election, but not a majority. Political violence and partisan deadlock ended in the military coup of 1980, which banned every party. The name CHP was banned until 1987. Successor parties formed, among them the Social Democratic Populist Party under Erdal İnönü, and on 9 September 1992 Deniz Baykal re-established the CHP under its original name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party's logo is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-six-arrows">Six Arrows</a>, which stand for the principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kemalism">Kemalism</a>: republicanism, reformism, laicism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/populism">populism</a>, nationalism and etatism. It is cited as the founding party of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and under its one-party rule it was the main instrument for carrying out far-reaching political, cultural, social and economic reforms. It is the oldest political party in Turkey. In the 2002 election it won 20 per cent of the vote and 32 per cent of the seats, because only it and the new AK Party passed the 10 per cent threshold.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Republican People's Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
